 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 18 \\\\  &  & -2 & 12 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & -6 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+2}={x}^{2}-6x+9\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)({x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(x-3)(x-3)=(x+2)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-2$ and a double root at $x=3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+2)(0-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,18),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557265 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557265 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557265 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557265 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557265 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ec517e9-4418-5776-9805-f9f4171a3217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557265 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557280 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557280 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557280 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557280 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $x-4$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-32x+32$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557280 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cca0427-a573-5822-8e99-bd72fe2c51d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557280 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 10 & -32 & 32 \\\\  &  & -4 & 24 & -32 \\\\  & -1 & 6 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-6x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)({x}^{2}-6x+8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)(x-2)(x-4)=-(x-2)(x-4)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)(0-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-2)(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,32),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/aa/aae66bc5dcf29e5e85e33942541b510d136b4f7f13d633f28c46d4b5a9ca977b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557316 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557316 #edb/ref 1069 "q-27994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557316 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557316 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(2-x)(1-x)^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de17e3d5-60e1-5541-9c18-3de6f1f6965c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557316 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(2-0)(1-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1,$ and\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-x)(1-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(2-x)({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/78/78af8854b6dd0da9eeded23cbcfa223018ca425d13d802b9e72039d906976bde.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557328 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557328 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557328 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557328 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x-4,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557328 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6ba8710-6e4b-5d1b-8f82-9bf7f2de8b0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557328 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 9 & -4 \\\\  &  & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\  & 1 & -5 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}-5x+4\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-5x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)(x-1)(x-4)=(x-4)(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-4)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557333 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557333 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557333 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557333 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $x+2$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+8x+12,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557333 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb4e0d8f-3854-5a60-be7f-5cac9da0624a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557333 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & -1 & -1 & 8 & 12 \\\\  &  & 2 & -2 & -12 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+2} & =-{x}^{2}+x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)(-{x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+2)({x}^{2}-x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+2)(x+2)(x-3)=-(x-3)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-3)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-3)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/99/99063a71f1a368c2f27bd9b4d3817ea8f39e34789d26074f70c68333667cd90f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557334 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff659825-07ff-583c-bc20-8ee310e82141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557334 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)(3+0)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,6),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/dc/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
